-- #1182 CONTROL — identical to main.mdk except the two `impl` blocks are SWAPPED.
-- `impl A1 (Q Int y z)` is declared first and `impl A2 (Q x Bool z)` second. Nothing else
-- changes: same two imported interfaces, same data type, same bodies, same call.
--
-- THE PAIR IS THE ASSERTION, not either file alone: together they pin (A2 first -> 2) AND
-- (A1 first -> 1), which IS the statement "the impl-block order decides". That is the
-- reordering-differential shape test/MUST-FAIL-NOT-PINNABLE.txt's #1183 entry argues a
-- SINGLE must-fail row cannot express — answered here by spending the CONTROL SLOT on the
-- second ordering rather than on an unrelated near-miss, so both orderings are graded.
--
-- ⚠️ 1 IS ALSO THE ANSWER A CORRECT COMPILER GIVES, and that is deliberate. It makes this
-- control DOUBLE as the post-fix regression guard: scoping the candidate set by interface
-- makes BOTH files print 1, so main.mdk's pinned stdout moves (a clean DRAINED) while this
-- control keeps passing. The shape this fixture replaced could not do that — its control
-- asserted the wrong-value ordering, so a fix produced a CONTROL-BROKE whose printed advice
-- ("the ENVIRONMENT moved, not the bug") was wrong.
--
-- Per #1144 this harness grades a control on its EXIT CODE ONLY, and a
-- wrong-value-at-exit-0 bug cannot be discriminated by an exit code unless the program puts
-- the value INTO the exit code. So `main` ASSERTS 1 and panics otherwise, rather than
-- printing it — a control that merely printed would exit 0 either way and could never fail.
import a1.{A1, m}
import a2.{A2}

data Q a b c = Q a b c

impl A1 (Q Int y z) where
  m _ = 1

impl A2 (Q x Bool z) where
  m _ = 2

main =
  let r = m (Q 1 True 7)
  if r == 1 then
    println "control-ok:1"
  else
    panic "control regressed: with impl A1 declared first the answer is no longer 1"
